/** @page xmlstreaming Streaming and SAX Processing

    @tableofcontents

    Converting a document with @ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml() "parse_xml()" or loading it into an
    @ref Qore::Xml::XmlDoc "XmlDoc" keeps the whole document in memory. For large documents, or documents arriving
    from a stream, the xml module provides a streaming reader and SAX iterators that process one node or one element
    at a time.

    @section xmlstreamingchoice Choosing an API

    |!Task|!API
    |Iterate over the repeated records of a large document, each record as %Qore data|\
        @ref Qore::Xml::SaxIterator "SaxIterator" (string), @ref Qore::Xml::FileSaxIterator "FileSaxIterator" \
        (file) or @ref Qore::Xml::InputStreamSaxIterator "InputStreamSaxIterator" (input stream)
    |Walk every node of a document, or convert selected subtrees to %Qore data|\
        @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader "XmlReader"
    |Validate a document while reading it|@ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader "XmlReader" (XML Schema or RELAX NG), and the \
        SAX iterators (XML Schema, with the \c xsd option); see @ref xmlschemavalidation
    |Read or write the records of XML documents through the data provider API|\
        @ref saxdataproviderintro "SaxDataProvider"

    @section xmlstreamingsax SAX Iterators

    A SAX iterator returns each element with the given name as %Qore data, converted as described in
    @ref xmlserialization, without building the whole document; only the current element is kept in memory. The
    iterators accept an \c encoding, the \c xml_parse_options parsing flags, an \c xsd schema to validate the
    document while iterating, and an \c xml_input_io callback to resolve its imports.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires xml
%requires FsUtil

TmpFile export();
export.file.write("<export><record id=\"1\"><total>10.50</total></record>"
    "<record id=\"2\"><total>99.95</total></record></export>");
export.file.close();

number sum = 0n;
FileSaxIterator records(export.path, "record");
foreach hash<auto> record in (records) {
    sum += record.total;
    printf("record %s: %s\n", record."^attributes^".id, record.total);
}
printf("sum: %s\n", sum);
    @endcode

    An input stream is iterated in the same way, here with schema validation while reading:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires xml

const Xsd = '<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <xs:element name="export"><xs:complexType><xs:sequence>
    <xs:element name="record" type="xs:int" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
  </xs:sequence></xs:complexType></xs:element>
</xs:schema>';

InputStreamSaxIterator records(new StringInputStream("<export><record>1</record><record>2</record></export>"),
    "record", {"xsd": Xsd});
map printf("record %d: %y\n", $#, $1), records;
    @endcode

    @section xmlstreamingreader Streaming Reader

    @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader "XmlReader" is a pull parser: each call to
    @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::read() "read()" moves to the next node, whose type, name, depth, value and attributes
    can then be queried. @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::toQore() "toQore()" converts the current element and its
    subtree to %Qore data and moves past it.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires xml

XmlReader reader("<orders><order id=\"71\"><item>A-100</item></order><order id=\"72\"/></orders>");
while (reader.read()) {
    if (reader.nodeType() == XML_NODE_TYPE_ELEMENT && reader.name() == "order") {
        printf("order %s at depth %d\n", reader.getAttribute("id"), reader.depth());
    }
}
    @endcode

    @section xmlstreamingdataprovider Data Provider

    The @ref saxdataproviderintro "SaxDataProvider" module provides the records of an XML document through the data
    provider API (searching and filtering records), and writes records as XML:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires xml
%requires FsUtil
%requires SaxDataProvider

TmpFile data();
data.file.write("<customers><customer><name>Acme</name><country>DE</country></customer>"
    "<customer><name>Globex</name><country>US</country></customer></customers>");
data.file.close();

SaxDataProvider provider({"location": data.path, "element_name": "customer"});
AbstractDataProviderRecordIterator i = provider.searchRecords({"country": "US"});
map printf("%s\n", $1.name), i;
    @endcode
*/
